| 145 | `AND` and `OR` evaluate both operands, so `(i < \|s\|) AND (s[i] = 'e')` with `i = \|s\|` raises `IndexOutOfBounds`; the conditional forms `AND:` and `OR:` short-circuit | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | deliberate (library) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/operators/conditionalops.tex:13-27`, "Conditional Operators" | `explorations/apl/rung-3/t03_and.fss` | run-c | A bare `a AND b` takes the strict `opr AND(a:Boolean, b:Boolean)` over `opr AND(a:Boolean, b:()->Boolean)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2649`, `:2651` at c124c6b65): in `t03_and.fss` (a), `false AND bump()` runs `bump` once, `false AND (fn (): Boolean => bump())` zero times; `true OR bump()` once. `OR`/`OR:` are the same pair (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2648`, `:2650`). Specified: only an operator followed by `:` is conditional, so every `while (i < n) AND (s[i] …)` loop trips on it. Run as `explorations/apl/rung-3/REPORT.md`, "How to run", says. Probe `explorations/run-c/probes/g145_and.fss`, parent of 4ebf80aec. Workaround: `AND:`, or a thunk. full text: history, row 145 |
